@*
    Translating - standing between two people who do not share a language.

    TWO HALVES, NOT A FORM. Each person owns a half. The top half is rotated 180
    degrees to face the person opposite, and the phone is meant to be put down on
    the table between them rather than held.

    IT LISTENS UNTIL YOU STOP IT. Tapping a microphone used to capture ONE
    utterance and stop, so a conversation was a sequence of taps and every turn
    waited for the phone to finish the last one. A conversation does not work
    like that. The mic now stays open: speech is cut on the lull that ends it,
    each piece is queued, and translation drains that queue BEHIND the talking.
    Somebody can say the next thing while the phone is still rendering the last.

    A PIPELINE, NOT THREE THREADS. Running several models at once is what this
    device cannot do - the answering model is serialised on purpose because two
    overlapping turns interleave their tokens, and a second Whisper state on a
    3.7 GB phone is how a rebuild turns into an out-of-memory kill. What makes it
    feel simultaneous is that CAPTURE and RENDERING are separate: the queue
    absorbs the difference, so the phone being slow stops being the same thing as
    the conversation being slow.

    WHOSE TURN IT IS, ASSUMED AND CORRECTABLE. The recogniser has to be told
    which language it is hearing - left to guess it writes Japanese down as
    English. So the side you start on speaks first and then it alternates, which
    is what two people taking turns actually do. When that assumption is wrong,
    tapping the other side's microphone puts it right without losing what has
    already been said.

    ITS OWN LANGUAGES, ITS OWN SCREEN. The pair belongs to this service, not to
    the app: somebody can run the app in English, keep their CV in Japanese, and
    translate between Korean and Mandarin.
*@
@page "/translate"
@implements IDisposable
@inject ISettings Settings
@inject ISpokenLanguage Spoken
@inject IWhereAmI Where
@inject IBrain Brain
@inject IConversation Talk
@inject IVoiceHost Voice
@inject IFormFactor Host
@inject NavigationManager Nav

<div class="talk">

    @* THEIRS. Rotated, so it reads the right way up from across the table. *@
    <section class="half half-top @(_listening && _expecting == Side.Top ? "half-live" : null)">
        @RenderHalf(Side.Top)
    </section>

    @*
        THE SEAM. A conversation has two ends and this is the table between them;
        it carries what belongs to neither side - swapping who sits where, what
        went wrong, what is still waiting, and what the last piece cost.
    *@
    <div class="seam">
        <button class="seam-swap" @onclick="Swap" aria-label="Swap the two sides">&#8646;</button>

        @if (_note is { } n)
        {
            <span class="seam-note">@n</span>
        }
        else if (_fetch is { } f)
        {
            <span class="seam-note">@f</span>
        }
        else if (_queued > 0)
        {
            @* The queue is the point, so it is visible: somebody who has said
               three things needs to see that all three are coming. *@
            <span class="seam-note">@_queued waiting</span>
        }
        else if (_timing is { } t)
        {
            <span class="seam-timing">@t</span>
        }
    </div>

    @* YOURS. *@
    <section class="half half-bottom @(_listening && _expecting == Side.Bottom ? "half-live" : null)">
        @RenderHalf(Side.Bottom)
    </section>

</div>

@code {
    // THE STORAGE KEY STAYS "interpret" though the product now says translate.
    // It is the key somebody's chosen pair is already saved under, and renaming
    // it would quietly forget the languages they set.
    const string Service = "interpret";

    enum Side { Top, Bottom }

    /// <summary>One thing somebody said, on the half it belongs to.</summary>
    /// <param name="Side">Whose half it shows on.</param>
    /// <param name="Text">The words, in that half's language.</param>
    /// <param name="Mine">
    /// True when this is what that person SAID, false when it is the other
    /// person's words rendered for them. Both belong on their half; only one of
    /// them came out of their mouth.
    /// </param>
    sealed record Line(Side Side, string Text, bool Mine);

    static IReadOnlyList<SampleLanguage> Languages { get; } = SampleLanguages.All.Values
        .OrderBy(l => l.Name, StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
        .ToList();

    // NO HARD-CODED PAIR. This was "zu" and "en", which is a fine guess in
    // Johannesburg and a useless one anywhere else - and Home carried the same
    // two constants, so the two screens had to agree by hand. Both now ask the
    // phone: your language, and the language of the people around you.
    /// <summary>Why this pair was suggested, in the words of the signal that said so.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Named rather than assumed: "Japanese, from this phone's timezone" is a
    /// claim somebody can check and correct. "isiZulu" from nowhere is not.
    /// </remarks>
    string? _pairReason;

    string _topLang = "";
    string _bottomLang = "";

    /// <summary>The conversation so far, oldest first.</summary>
    readonly List<Line> _lines = new();

    /// <summary>Heard but not yet rendered - the queue that absorbs the wait.</summary>
    readonly Queue<(Side Side, string Text)> _pending = new();
    int _queued;

    /// <summary>Which side the microphone is listening for.</summary>
    Side _expecting = Side.Bottom;

    bool _listening;
    bool _draining;

    /// <summary>True while the phone is talking, so it does not record itself.</summary>
    bool _speaking;

    CancellationTokenSource? _capture;

    Side? _typing;
    string _draft = "";

    string? _note;
    string? _fetch;
    string? _timing;
    bool _cold = true;

    /// <summary>A duration, in the shortest honest unit.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// INVARIANT, because this phone is set to a comma-decimal locale and the
    /// first run of this line printed "9,4s". A measurement is compared, pasted
    /// and searched for; it should read the same wherever it is taken.
    /// </remarks>
    static string Secs(long ms) => ms >= 1000
        ? (ms / 1000.0).ToString("0.0", System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture) + "s"
        : ms.ToString(System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture) + "ms";

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        // A REMEMBERED PAIR WINS, because it was chosen. Only a first run
        // falls through to what the phone can work out.
        var (mine, theirs, why) = LanguageSuggestion.Pair(Spoken.Current, Where.Locate());
        _pairReason = why;

        _bottomLang = await Settings.ServiceSettingAsync(Service, "from", mine) ?? mine;
        _topLang = await Settings.ServiceSettingAsync(Service, "to", theirs ?? mine) ?? theirs ?? mine;
    }

    string LangOf(Side s) => s == Side.Top ? _topLang : _bottomLang;
    static Side Other(Side s) => s == Side.Top ? Side.Bottom : Side.Top;
    static string Name(string tag) => SampleLanguages.Find(tag)?.Name ?? tag;

    IEnumerable<Line> LinesFor(Side s) => _lines.Where(l => l.Side == s);

    async Task SetLang(Side s, string tag)
    {
        if (s == Side.Top) _topLang = tag; else _bottomLang = tag;
        await Settings.SetServiceSettingAsync(Service, s == Side.Top ? "to" : "from", tag);

        // AND FETCH THE VOICE, because choosing a language the phone cannot speak
        // is not a choice - it is a silence somebody discovers mid-conversation.
        _ = EnsureVoiceAsync(tag);
    }

    /// <summary>Put this language's voice on the phone if it is not already.</summary>
    async Task EnsureVoiceAsync(string tag)
    {
        try
        {
            // Bytes is the size of the download this language WOULD need, so a
            // null means it is already here and there is nothing to fetch.
            var rows = await Voice.CatalogueAsync();
            var row = rows.FirstOrDefault(r =>
                string.Equals(r.Tag, tag, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase));
            if (row?.Bytes is not long bytes) return;

            var name = Name(tag);
            _fetch = $"Getting the {name} voice - {bytes / 1_000_000} MB";
            await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);

            var progress = new Progress<string>(line =>
            {
                _fetch = $"{name}: {line}";
                InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
            });

            var outcome = await Voice.SpeakAsync(tag, progress);
            _fetch = outcome.Spoke ? null : $"No {name} voice: {outcome.Detail}";
        }
        catch (Exception ex) { _fetch = Plain(ex); }
        finally { await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged); }
    }

    /// <summary>Turn the phone around: the pair swap seats, and so does the talk.</summary>
    async Task Swap()
    {
        (_topLang, _bottomLang) = (_bottomLang, _topLang);
        for (var i = 0; i < _lines.Count; i++)
            _lines[i] = _lines[i] with { Side = Other(_lines[i].Side) };
        _expecting = Other(_expecting);
        await Settings.SetServiceSettingAsync(Service, "to", _topLang);
        await Settings.SetServiceSettingAsync(Service, "from", _bottomLang);
    }

    /// <summary>Start listening for this side, hand the turn over, or stop.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The side you tap speaks FIRST, and the same button is how a wrong guess is
    /// corrected mid-conversation: tapping the other microphone says "my turn"
    /// without discarding anything already said.
    /// </remarks>
    void Listen(Side speaker)
    {
        if (_listening && _expecting == speaker) { Stop(); return; }

        _expecting = speaker;
        _note = null;

        if (_listening) return;          // already running; only the turn moved

        _listening = true;
        _capture = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _ = CaptureLoopAsync(_capture.Token);
    }

    void Stop()
    {
        _listening = false;
        _capture?.Cancel();
    }

    /// <summary>Keep the microphone open, cutting on each lull, until stopped.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// DictateAsync already ends a capture on silence, so the segmenting was
    /// free - what was missing was going round again. Nothing here waits for a
    /// translation: the piece is queued and the loop returns to listening, which
    /// is the whole reason the conversation can run ahead of the phone.
    /// </remarks>
    async Task CaptureLoopAsync(CancellationToken ct)
    {
        try
        {
            while (_listening && !ct.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // NOT WHILE IT IS TALKING. An open microphone during playback
                // records the phone's own voice and translates it straight back.
                while (_speaking && !ct.IsCancellationRequested)
                    await Task.Delay(120, ct).ConfigureAwait(false);
                if (ct.IsCancellationRequested) break;

                var side = _expecting;
                var heard = "";
                var updates = new Progress<TurnState>(t =>
                {
                    if (t.Heard is { Length: > 0 } h) heard = h;
                });

                try
                {
                    await Talk.DictateAsync(updates, ct, LangOf(side));
                }
                catch (OperationCanceledException) { break; }
                catch (Exception ex)
                {
                    _note = Plain(ex);
                    await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
                    break;
                }

                var said = heard.Trim();
                if (said.Length == 0) continue;      // a lull with nothing in it

                _lines.Add(new Line(side, said, Mine: true));
                _pending.Enqueue((side, said));
                _queued = _pending.Count;

                // TURN-TAKING, ASSUMED. Two people alternate; when they do not,
                // the other microphone is one tap away.
                _expecting = Other(side);
                await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);

                _ = DrainAsync();
            }
        }
        finally
        {
            _listening = false;
            await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
        }
    }

    /// <summary>Render everything waiting, one at a time, behind the talking.</summary>
    async Task DrainAsync()
    {
        if (_draining) return;
        _draining = true;
        try
        {
            while (_pending.Count > 0)
            {
                var (side, text) = _pending.Dequeue();
                _queued = _pending.Count;
                await RenderAsync(side, text);
            }
        }
        finally { _draining = false; }
    }

    /// <summary>One piece: translate it, put it on the other half, say it.</summary>
    async Task RenderAsync(Side speaker, string said)
    {
        var clock = System.Diagnostics.Stopwatch.StartNew();
        var brain = await Brain.StateAsync();
        var checkMs = clock.ElapsedMilliseconds;

        if (!brain.Ready)
        {
            _note = brain.Detail;
            await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
            return;
        }

        var fromName = Name(LangOf(speaker));
        var toName = Name(LangOf(Other(speaker)));

        try
        {
            // Asked as a translation, not as a conversation: without "give only
            // the translation" the model ANSWERS the sentence, which is a very
            // different thing to put in front of somebody at a hospital desk.
            clock.Restart();
            var rendered = (await Brain.AskAsync(
                $"Translate this from {fromName} to {toName}. "
              + $"Give only the translation, nothing else:\n\n{said}")).Trim();
            var translateMs = clock.ElapsedMilliseconds;

            _lines.Add(new Line(Other(speaker), rendered, Mine: false));
            await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);

            // SPOKEN, NOT JUST SHOWN. The other person may not read, which is the
            // assumption this whole app exists to stop making.
            _speaking = true;
            SpeakOutcome? spoke = null;
            try { spoke = await Voice.SayAsync(LangOf(Other(speaker)), rendered); }
            catch { /* a mute phone still shows the words */ }
            finally { _speaking = false; }

            var legs = new List<string>(4)
            {
                $"check {Secs(checkMs)}",
                $"render {Secs(translateMs)}{(_cold ? " (cold)" : "")}",
            };
            if (spoke?.Milliseconds is { } synth)
            {
                legs.Add($"synth {Secs(synth)}");
                if (spoke.AudioMilliseconds is { } audio) legs.Add($"audio {Secs(audio)}");
            }
            _timing = string.Join(" - ", legs);
            _cold = false;
        }
        catch (Exception ex) { _note = Plain(ex); }
        finally { await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged); }
    }

    /// <summary>Put a typed line into the same queue the microphone feeds.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// ONE PIPELINE, TWO WAYS IN. A typed line that skipped the queue could
    /// overtake spoken ones and land out of order in the transcript.
    /// </remarks>
    async Task Send(Side speaker)
    {
        var said = _draft.Trim();
        if (said.Length == 0) return;

        _draft = "";
        _typing = null;
        _lines.Add(new Line(speaker, said, Mine: true));
        _pending.Enqueue((speaker, said));
        _queued = _pending.Count;
        _expecting = Other(speaker);
        await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
        _ = DrainAsync();
    }

    void OpenKeyboard(Side s)
    {
        _typing = s;
        _draft = "";
    }

    /// <summary>One person's half: their side of the conversation, and the ways in.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Both halves are the SAME markup - the top one is only rotated by CSS.
    /// Writing them separately would let them drift, and the whole point is that
    /// neither person is the second-class one with a smaller set of controls.
    /// </remarks>
    RenderFragment RenderHalf(Side s) => @<div class="half-inner">

        @* THE CONVERSATION, not just the latest line. Newest last; the CSS keeps
           the newest against the seam, which is where each person is looking. *@
        <div class="half-lines">
            @foreach (var line in LinesFor(s))
            {
                <div class="line @(line.Mine ? "line-mine" : "line-theirs")" dir="auto">@line.Text</div>
            }
            @if (_draining && !LinesFor(s).Any())
            {
                <div class="line line-waiting">...</div>
            }
        </div>

        @if (_typing == s)
        {
            <div class="half-compose">
                <textarea class="half-type" rows="2" placeholder="Type it"
                          aria-label="What to translate"
                          @bind="_draft" @bind:event="oninput"></textarea>
                <button class="half-go" disabled="@(_draft.Trim().Length == 0)"
                        @onclick="() => Send(s)">Send</button>
            </div>
        }
        else
        {
            <div class="half-controls">
                @if (Host.IsOnDevice)
                {
                    @* ONE TAP STARTS THE CONVERSATION, not one utterance. While it
                       is listening this side's mic stops it; the other side's says
                       "my turn" and corrects a wrong guess. *@
                    <button class="mic @(_listening && _expecting == s ? "mic-live" : null)"
                            @onclick="() => Listen(s)"
                            aria-label="@(_listening && _expecting == s ? "Stop listening" : "Speak " + Name(LangOf(s)))">
                        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="30" height="30" fill="none"
                             stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8"
                             stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
                            <rect x="9" y="2" width="6" height="11" rx="3" />
                            <path d="M5 11a7 7 0 0 0 14 0" />
                            <path d="M12 18v4" />
                        </svg>
                    </button>
                }
                <button class="keys" @onclick="() => OpenKeyboard(s)">Type</button>
            </div>
        }

        <select class="lang" aria-label="This side's language"
                @bind:get="LangOf(s)" @bind:set="v => SetLang(s, v)">
            @foreach (var l in Languages)
            {
                <option value="@l.Tag">@l.Name</option>
            }
        </select>
    </div>;

    /// <summary>A failure said in words, not in the name of a .NET type.</summary>
    static string Plain(Exception ex) => ex switch
    {
        HttpRequestException => "Could not reach the internet.",
        IOException => "Not enough space on the phone.",
        _ => "That did not work. Try it again.",
    };

    /// <summary>Leaving the screen stops the microphone.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// An open capture that outlives its page holds the one-turn semaphore for
    /// the life of the process, and every later press of a microphone anywhere in
    /// the app then returns instantly and silently.
    /// </remarks>
    public void Dispose()
    {
        _listening = false;
        _capture?.Cancel();
        _capture?.Dispose();
    }
}
